Z||000038||batboy||12-22-2000||09:36 PM||||With all due respect guys, it's over...don't fan the coals back up into flames. You are all entitled to your own opinions of course. I will let things slide the first time or two. BUT, this was not the first time Archie attacked my Forum friends over petty crap. Look at the above posts. I didn't tell anyone to "Screw you", Archie did. If that isn't a flame, then nothing is.<P><BR>I've had lots of private emails supporting and agreeing with me. They just choose to stay out of the fray. Was I being immature? Of course I was! I said earlier that I responded in kind. I wasn't the only one acting childish here. If you want to start a "Batboy is so immature" topic, then go to the O/T section and do so. If you don't like what I have to say, then don't read my posts, simple as that. Let's get this technical section back to being technical.||||reg||1
Z||000039||gixxer||12-22-2000||11:36 PM||||OK Batboy. Let's get technical.<BR>In fact, let's get down and dirty Chicken s**t style. <BR>Fiero do not suffer from torque steer. They can't. They're not front wheel drive.<BR>The problem you are refering to is called "Bushing Deflection Steer".<BR>Torque steer is when a CV joint or U-joint from the half shaft, is angled in any direction but straight, and is rotating with a load applied to it.<BR>These secondary loads that arise from the U-joints agony are applied to each wheel hub in essentially all directions. The up and down loads are handled by the lower ball joints and the upper B/J or strut. The loads in the side to side directions are fed into the steering arms, or whatever links are used to hold the wheel straight ahead.<BR>If the outer CV joints on either side of the car are at, or are deflected to the same angle, the torque steer moment is cancelled out and the driver receives no steering wheel twisting input. If however, the CV/U-joints have different angles,  there will be a net difference in the moment input into the steering linkage, and the steering wheel will twist accordingly.<BR>The Fiero does not have a steering wheel attached to the rear upright, ergo, it will not experience "torque steer".<BR>But I knew what you meant anyway, so all is forgiven..=)<BR>Peace, <BR>Brian<BR>   ||||reg||12
Z||000040||Cooter||12-23-2000||12:09 AM||||Why is it that when I punch my 88GT it pulls to the right and when I let off it pulls to the left but at steady speeds it tracks perfectly straight? BTW- new poly bushings in rear, 4 wheel alighnment and new tires properly inflated. I can't figure this one out.||||reg||1
Z||000041||JohnnyK||12-23-2000||02:27 AM||||Aight hugh, so be it. I respect that. I also think I should get an answer to my question though. It IS out of curiosity. Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000042||Will||12-23-2000||03:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cooter:<BR><B>Why is it that when I punch my 88GT it pulls to the right and when I let off it pulls to the left but at steady speeds it tracks perfectly straight? BTW- new poly bushings in rear, 4 wheel alighnment and new tires properly inflated. I can't figure this one out.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My '86 SE V6 5 spd did that--rev it up, shift hard, and it would blip to the left with every shift. I eventually was able to drive it straight, then I drove my dad's car, and it blipped to the right every time I shifted  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>I think that is caused by worn axle CV joints. How many miles on your car? The unequal length axle setup is not ideal. Supposedly an extension shaft from a Beretta will help this (and make replacement right axles a HELL of a lot easier to find!)||||reg||1
